#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064992} :activity/id #uuid "8a6775e9-2229-5837-a376-b69fe905d97e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048123 :topic/id #uuid "80ecc4ad-075a-51a6-a1fc-72dc61a2444d" :topic/math-academy-id 313} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065347 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ on the closed interval $[0,7]$ shown below.\n\n![](images/39/39851b8fc5658ccfd92711eb9fe7a00a730300a3bf2e635911bdd51ea69de37a.png)\n\nThe **critical points** of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=4,$ there is a sharp corner, so $f'(x)$ doesn't exist.\n\nSo, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=2$ and $x=4.$ \n\nIn particular, a critical point is called a **stationary point** if $f'(x)=0.$ So here, the points $x=2$ and $x=4$ are both critical points, but only the point at $x=2$ is a stationary point.\n\n![](images/4e/4e91994fa85a068ef847cbd82dc176569fd15ab2487dfff6936280e9a1deb634.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "36584e6c-f116-5ad4-bb03-576b0f7e9c0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02673d5b-6611-5854-bca0-ff7445f2f3c3" :step/math-academy-id 12782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064044 :question/id #uuid "2d9fc36f-8d8f-5e57-9ff0-28a032e71980" :question/math-academy-id "e-3726"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402909}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "2b2aa51f-4124-5395-afb4-05149fddd969" :step/math-academy-id 12789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064116 :question/id #uuid "97897d09-3dc9-5e35-8f56-d44f54c542cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6169"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403640}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "589482bf-a622-576c-8b59-1c5332c13087" :step/math-academy-id 23042 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065374 :tutorial/content "Some critical points are **local extrema**. There are two types of local extrema:\n\n- A **local minimum** is the lowest point of a valley in a graph. Equivalently, $x=a$ is a local minimum if for $x$ values \"close\" to $x=a,$ we have $f(x) \\geq f(a)$.\n\n- A **local maximum** is the highest point of a peak in a graph. Equivalently, $x=a$ is a local maximum if for $x$ values \"close\" to $x=a,$ we have $f(x) \\leq f(a)$.\n\n![](images/d6/d6fbdf74b282bd5b8172fcc7927f84bbdfd050a717794f3b40c8e58b83d7b87e.png)\n\nOn the graph above, the critical point at $x=4$ is a local minimum since it is the lowest point of a valley. Likewise, the critical point at $x=6$ is a local maximum since it is the highest point of a peak. But the critical point at $x=2$ is not a local extremum since it is neither a peak nor a valley.\n\nThe **global extrema** of a function in an interval are given by the lowest or highest points of the function's graph in the whole interval.\n\n- The lowest point is the **global minimum**, whereas\n\n- the highest point is the **global maximum**.\n\nHere, the global minimum is at $x=4$, and the global maximum is at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/8a/8adb83da866dae1cd122b5271b2cb9036c72a389eaf1c48148888140bda576ac.png)\n\n\"Local\" extrema are the lowest or highest points compared to points that are very close to them. On the other hand, \"global\" extrema are the lowest/highest points of the graph over its entire domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "05856315-caeb-577d-9dea-b5e5f8830820" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3898 :tutorial/title "Local and Global Extrema of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "b952eadc-3fe2-5be0-8961-f9af3dc6fcd5" :step/math-academy-id 23043 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064993}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064409}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060808} :activity/id #uuid "37dbb8fe-57e2-5578-a0d2-de00af96f5ed" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146 :topic/id #uuid "832923c0-f0e3-599f-af85-37cb7dae2658" :topic/math-academy-id 153} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061006 :tutorial/content "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & ={{(\\,7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{11}}{\\overset{⏞}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|}}-{{(\\,1\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{12}}{\\overset{⏞}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|}}+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{13}}{\\overset{⏞}{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $[\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{aligned}]$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{aligned}| & =a|\\begin{aligned}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{aligned}|-b|\\begin{aligned}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{aligned}|+c|\\begin{aligned}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" :tutorial/id #uuid "99828b7b-b501-5509-8853-d17316824f5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9175 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "57f8ef60-6efd-5887-929c-a96714976291" :step/math-academy-id 464292 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059452 :question/id #uuid "14e05d4f-373c-5d1f-aa59-7ee52466480e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14762"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048965}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364948}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8e5e1cdf-9076-58fb-b468-401ba770db35" :step/math-academy-id 464293 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060810}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061007 :tutorial/content "If the determinant of a square matrix $A$ is equal to zero, then we say that $A$ is a **singular** matrix.\n\nIf a matrix is singular, then it does not have a multiplicative inverse." :tutorial/id #uuid "2dc6054b-777f-5106-9e65-71bede9f6b49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9176 :tutorial/title "Singular Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "e038964f-9424-5512-b66c-59d652b56e87" :step/math-academy-id 464296 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059453 :question/id #uuid "d7516cec-bd96-59db-98cd-5d82e41fa719" :question/math-academy-id "e-14763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403335}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "2bf05412-402c-54cb-80bb-3ec55c979a73" :step/math-academy-id 464297 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061008 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the $3\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthat has an unknown constant $k$ as one of the entries, and we are given that $A$ is singular. How do we find all the possible values of $k?$\n\nOne way is to use the formula for the determinant of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k,$ and then make an equation with the given determinant to solve for $k.$\n\nSo first, let's use the formula for the determinant on our matrix to get an expression in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1-2)-2(k-0)+3(2k-0) \\\\  & =-3-2k+6k \\\\  & =4k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we are told that $\\det(A) =0,$ let's equate the above expression to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ 4k-3 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only possible value for $k$ is $\\dfrac{3}{4}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "55643210-75d5-50e5-a63b-30b951b8a201" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9177 :tutorial/title "Solving for a Variable"} :step/id #uuid "1db34187-4316-57e4-9ac9-a00c7f32f091" :step/math-academy-id 464300 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059454 :question/id #uuid "7780340c-43d6-5d38-9270-865914098218" :question/math-academy-id "e-14764"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400366}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "661a91c2-23f6-5cc7-bb2a-4955ef3942cb" :step/math-academy-id 464301}] :activity/title "The Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059886}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084191} :activity/id #uuid "e40e1898-52bd-590d-8c30-6678bae371ba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317 :topic/id #uuid "9438800f-fe1e-5478-b0c5-e3b869cf359e" :topic/math-academy-id 730} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084464 :tutorial/content "The **expected value** (also known as the **expectation** or **mean**) of a discrete random variable is the average value we'd expect to get if we observed the random variable many times.\n\nFor a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nIn other words, the expected value is the sum of products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, taken over all possible values of $X.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1cdabf3-eea2-5288-b192-5ba380e68539" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3849 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dc31fde7-31d0-506e-8116-119fcccac42d" :step/math-academy-id 22375 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084192}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083065 :question/id #uuid "27a80de6-49d0-557b-8444-ebab046e795b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6074"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "332fefd8-7de7-5a84-81e2-a2aa97be9b73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400310}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value for a Random Variable with Uniform Probability"} :step/id #uuid "85a8eef0-559d-5f4f-8493-97743760f755" :step/math-academy-id 22376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084242}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083066 :question/id #uuid "2b946455-893f-57db-b0a5-d52091914cf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6075"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90d0c882-c94b-5759-9a78-63979365e318" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400317}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Profit of a Game"} :step/id #uuid "3bb059cd-67b3-5071-80b0-6ffd0cb9641b" :step/math-academy-id 22377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083308 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083108 :question/id #uuid "e6dd60de-5df1-531d-9971-847343303fd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7383"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8297713-fb86-59f2-af4d-850af2575541" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371194}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value for a Random Variable with Non-Uniform Probability"} :step/id #uuid "cb1f9817-259a-5311-8321-58e35da5620c" :step/math-academy-id 27376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083309 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083109 :question/id #uuid "ed80b546-956c-5b67-ac7d-0c0049c2278c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c3fc090-22e5-5f05-9634-b8c645cf5700" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371201}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value Given the Frequency of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "908668b1-aa6a-5aac-95b8-26d8ecadb1bc" :step/math-academy-id 27386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084193}}] :activity/title "Expected Values of Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083425}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064988} :activity/id #uuid "b3e69442-b9dc-56f2-b6f4-b2d9169e67ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048434 :topic/id #uuid "9ef976b2-cf49-59ec-9689-0ec6f4a92d3c" :topic/math-academy-id 288} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065346 :tutorial/content "The **mean value theorem** states that for any function that is continuous and differentiable on some interval, there is some point where the derivative of the function is equal to the slope between the endpoints. More precisely, that is:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.\n> $$\n\nLet's illustrate this idea with an example. Consider the graph of the function \n\n$$\ny = f(x) = 6 - \\dfrac 4 x\n$$\n\nthat's defined on the closed interval $[1,4].$\n\n![](images/fd/fd4d75f2ed0db427c2e3bd41335ceb302c3126781f595c8c21ec914e50f82f64.png)\n\nThe line $l_1$ is the secant line that intersects the curve at the endpoints of the interval. The mean value theorem guarantees that there is some special point inside the interval where the tangent line has the same slope as $l_1.$ \n\nIn the diagram above, the special point is $(2,4),$ and the tangent line $l_2$ at this point is parallel to the secant line $l_1.$\n\nTo double-check that $l_1$ and $l_2$ are parallel, we can compute their slopes. The line $l_1$ passes through the points $(1,f(1))$ and $(4,f(4)),$ so its slope is given by \n\n$$\nm=\\dfrac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} =  \\dfrac{5-2}{4-1} = 1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the slope of the line $l_2$ can be found by calculating $f'(2).$\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{4}{x^2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(2) = 1\n$$\n\nSince the lines $l_1$ and $l_2$ have the same slope, they are parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "baf78b35-04c6-5d1c-913f-e0a3da70cd8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2659 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "10d30ee9-1327-5e6c-8d54-1b994e2a9962" :step/math-academy-id 12770 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064041 :question/id #uuid "4bbaf6b0-1057-5485-a3d8-2126e74fecdc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3717"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370618}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "50436e6a-bace-51f7-97a8-73a9025692ab" :step/math-academy-id 12771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064991}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064042 :question/id #uuid "ed796d54-235b-5ec0-b73f-5396d1ef99b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3718"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050572}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341952}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "9aa3d544-dbe8-5881-bfea-bb08af9d7f85" :step/math-academy-id 12772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064161 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064043 :question/id #uuid "d47e7afd-bed0-5f2b-8635-ddd1a5303837" :question/math-academy-id "e-3719"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392649}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "95f45e70-98bb-552e-8394-58e2d30a7385" :step/math-academy-id 12774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065167}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064123 :question/id #uuid "92bba9e0-62db-5c96-8cbc-3cfbe8863a94" :question/math-academy-id "e-6738"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403919}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "4737b469-c8a4-5051-b73e-2b3bd7b686fa" :step/math-academy-id 24870}] :activity/title "The Mean Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064394}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093060} :activity/id #uuid "2179f3df-5754-599f-a160-da910b72c2ff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048712 :topic/id #uuid "b784cbd9-6f0d-5689-9a0e-6e19dfca34c7" :topic/math-academy-id 931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093431 :tutorial/content "Consider once again the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square that is spanned by these vectors.\n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/8f/8fca8cf6dd5b232d3aed6eeb98fb6c244872c55425673cf3ac1441a22e2e29f3.svg) \n\nAs before, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/3f/3f8b5f0f827d359f32a4311021dc48947447133918d48a2f1cad511531b13c81.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/41/4195c8cd2f22fb0f84056226fb7b28c5f9944f747ca3230bf187752829b2a04e.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "70a75b2e-3fa9-5aa9-b308-79b56d57fae7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3542 :tutorial/title "Rotations of 90 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "f91bce30-93ef-5342-8485-0619a059a2b3" :step/math-academy-id 20041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092065 :question/id #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420105}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "7452acd6-f07a-5cbb-a6a1-887429d397a8" :step/math-academy-id 20045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093432 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/c6/c6865c5373231451885b3d76e3c8151e9c707462a8b405d9b014b1bda17065a8.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n![](images/5b/5b8e1c8e03ebb60716eaa2fefded5e7fc286f6de9cffdece8200f6f56c2bbe8e.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c2dc9f76bb23225cec0a4f62541d8a74c4c4b16d2e2f7c37e8d36922d97c59f0.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) about the origin.**\n\nNote that we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "410fc9b7-ab2d-52ba-9b26-7afd34fdf9e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3545 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "68d9dc46-2592-5260-a1bb-53e6b885e53f" :step/math-academy-id 20046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092066 :question/id #uuid "e945271e-2180-559b-9998-53ab9982043c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5458"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050589}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354275}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "9915017c-1452-5136-bb94-65860a727a47" :step/math-academy-id 20047 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093058}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093434 :tutorial/content "Finally, let \n\n![](images/1b/1b840cbdb5b3953dfef9910d34248ea280bf38299e4f0a3d0a78143e7301f159.svg)\n\nbe a matrix of a linear transformation ![](images/2d/2d90be63023ff97e21e1a97ccee6c387437ac2a99787278c6f71f37e3ac86850.svg) As usual, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/76/76e24611a2cd8a3d5d01fc0a04a9b7a3d0957db84dd1a45bc2f1f82492fb2efd.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0f/0fc5f33d0bd7047ab02bd9bc3ea3b00c083ab214f3bc8a51e9cbb6e54dc6b51b.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "49541094-17cb-52e7-bb58-8ee2199ad6d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3547 :tutorial/title "Rotations of 270 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "d48d44d1-fe67-587c-a56e-251eee6b68f8" :step/math-academy-id 20053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093135}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092114 :question/id #uuid "7091b406-96d1-5663-9575-66f26c6a6267" :question/math-academy-id "e-7186"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394284}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "496d3d86-9264-56e9-a8a9-63d5cf7428bf" :step/math-academy-id 26469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093059}}] :activity/title "Right-Angle Rotations as Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092430}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079486} :activity/id #uuid "2354fce6-93a9-515d-b710-3e2de7832a33" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048830 :topic/id #uuid "c41b4d47-6d5e-556b-9c16-aee07d9ba17b" :topic/math-academy-id 677} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079660 :tutorial/content "Suppose that, for an arithmetic series, we are only given\n\n- the sum $S_N$ of the first $N$ terms, and\n\n- the last term $a_N.$\n\nIf we want to find the value of the first term of the series, $a_1,$ we can use the following formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{N}{2}(a_1 +a_N).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose we have an arithmetic series where the sum of the first $N=20$ terms is $S_{20}=-320$ and the last term is $a_{20}=-35.$ To find the value of the first term, we can substitute these values into the formula above and solve for $a_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-320 & =\\frac{20}{2}({a}_{1}+(-35)) \\\\ -320 & =10({a}_{1}-35) \\\\ \\frac{-320}{10} & ={a}_{1}-35 \\\\ -32 & ={a}_{1}-35 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c09f8726-259b-5979-bdee-184b68781081" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2861 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "47407432-1530-538d-b263-6a0595278c4d" :step/math-academy-id 456059 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078548 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078300 :question/id #uuid "8d663147-d71b-5349-bb5b-c737d73d4d7e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4206"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d7a21aa-f2e3-5461-81a6-5cbd0f63c03d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048687}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326755}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given the Last Term and the Sum"} :step/id #uuid "363a87c5-002d-5e4d-8132-37243fe49929" :step/math-academy-id 456060 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079662 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that, for an arithmetic series, we are only given\n\n- the sum $S_N$ of the first $N$ terms, and\n\n- the common difference $d.$\n\nIf we want to find the value of the first term of the series, $a_1,$ we can use the following formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{N}{2}\\big( 2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose we have an arithmetic series where the sum of the first $N=10$ terms is $S_{10}=55,$ and the common difference is $d=3.$ To find the value of the first term, we can substitute these values into the formula above and solve for $a_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}55 & =\\frac{10}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(10-1)3) \\\\ 55 & =5(2{a}_{1}+27) \\\\ 11 & =2{a}_{1}+27 \\\\ -16 & =2{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e44c6e8e-22a9-5f57-a6cf-e8724bc5dc2a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2888 :tutorial/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series when the Last Term is Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "65c6e96c-6d14-5f05-bf4d-34454345ce03" :step/math-academy-id 456063 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079489}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078549 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078301 :question/id #uuid "9e118fb4-4787-5ed1-9ab9-13cfce102726" :question/math-academy-id "e-4207"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f9251ee-4d19-5074-a2a3-07e13149260f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050157}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332937}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given the Common Difference and the Sum"} :step/id #uuid "ee924c6c-b406-5aa7-b2ce-1cfd42017ae2" :step/math-academy-id 456064 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079490}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078550 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078302 :question/id #uuid "4de8d7af-50a7-5e9d-8f5d-908d6a06e174" :question/math-academy-id "e-4208"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07a3dfec-3f7b-5820-9be5-12bf4acd5a3b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345212}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given a Term and the Sum"} :step/id #uuid "457b35e9-5f43-5fa6-b9da-3890eca9d47d" :step/math-academy-id 456067}] :activity/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078692}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064998} :activity/id #uuid "d7ed3da1-763f-52e9-8a6e-ef8889f66e02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883 :topic/id #uuid "c96abbae-0759-5f81-9064-a4b37d0b7422" :topic/math-academy-id 339} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065350 :tutorial/content "We can use the **second derivative test** to determine whether a stationary point is a local (or relative) maximum or a local (or relative) minimum. \n\nThe second derivative test involves the following two steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the stationary points of the function, that is, the points where $f'(x)=0.$\n\n**Step 2:** Find the second derivative $f''(x)$ and use it to classify the stationary points according to the \"second derivative test\" as follows:\n\n- If $f''(x)< 0$ at a stationary point, then that stationary point is a local maximum (because a concave-down function looks like a downward-opening parabola near the stationary point).\n\n![](images/47/47deacbdaf8c58dd7a9500990581ba1e9e16411cc1ff6db5993e61e93e5de019.png)\n\n- If $f''(x) > 0$ at the stationary point, then that stationary point is a local minimum (because a concave-up function looks like an upward-opening parabola near the stationary point).\n\n![](images/4d/4d9612d463d8ee5cfc44d2673f5bfab8181300a4abfdfa03f5d3f7ebec3ed9f8.png)\n\nIn essence, the second derivative test uses the function's concavity at the stationary point to decide whether it's a local maximum or a local minimum. \n\nLet's use the second derivative test to determine the local maxima and minima of $f(x)= 6xe^{-x}.$\n\n**Step 1:** Find the stationary points of the function. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =0 \\\\ {(6x{e}^{-x})}^{'} & =0 \\\\ 6{e}^{-x}-6x{e}^{-x} & =0 \\\\ -6{e}^{-x}(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a single stationary point $x=1.$\n\n**Step 2:**  Find the second derivative $f''(x)$ and use it to classify the stationary points according to the second derivative test. Computing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(-6{e}^{-x}(x-1))}^{'} \\\\  & =6{e}^{-x}(x-1)-6{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =6{e}^{-x}(x-2) \\\\ {f}^{''}(1) & =-\\frac{6}{e}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f''(1)< 0,$ we conclude that $f$ has a **local maximum** at $x=1.$ Indeed, this matches up with what we see in the graph:\n\n![](images/ac/ac3a5bf8362222d669b2c5e7f350401353fddb1a3b8b94b0fe2895c0792f27e2.png)\n\n**Warning**: Sometimes, the second derivative test gives $f''(x) = 0$ at the stationary point. When this happens, it means that the second derivative test has *failed*, and we should use the first derivative test instead." :tutorial/id #uuid "cf7c2358-c5f5-5e5f-9bd3-0cfa73b7c0ff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2671 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "77ce33ce-8cac-5f3e-ba77-6f64823d352d" :step/math-academy-id 12806 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064047 :question/id #uuid "4f78dc34-7cc7-5024-9641-b4ea74e4d155" :question/math-academy-id "e-3743"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290567}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"} :step/id #uuid "f5486277-27fb-57ea-a545-7997fb51cd34" :step/math-academy-id 12807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064048 :question/id #uuid "088bc8a4-e82a-586b-89b5-49482f579c4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3744"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050120}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290428}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"} :step/id #uuid "3b10801c-b685-594c-8caa-c404be91fca5" :step/math-academy-id 12808 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064999}}] :activity/title "The Second Derivative Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064430}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088514} :activity/id #uuid "0c5859d1-4ff1-5efe-90ef-d19961e8bcc4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/id #uuid "ca183854-66cd-5b1f-9d29-03785c3216b8" :topic/math-academy-id 861} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088889 :tutorial/content "In mathematics, rectangular arrays containing numbers are called **matrices**. \n\nAs an example, consider the matrix $A$ below:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis particular matrix has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. The numbers in the matrix are called **entries** or **elements.**\n\nThe number of rows and columns in a matrix does not need to be the same. For example, consider another matrix that has $3$ rows and $4$ columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 13 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe **dimensions** of the matrix are the number of rows and the number of columns. For example, the matrix $M$ shown above has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, we write the dimensions of $M$ as $3 \\times 4$ (read as \"three by four\")." :tutorial/id #uuid "d68f01b8-8bc6-59a1-8d80-d284abbcdf6e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2968 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22697b73-625d-545a-ba00-223291b29933" :step/math-academy-id 16069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :question/id #uuid "6587b5f1-8a44-556a-a95e-20c54dfebc04" :question/math-academy-id "e-4367"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6ae25fc-16eb-598b-a288-742e4b46cb12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335982}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "b1b7d3eb-f611-589c-99d3-92b8c28fd2c4" :step/math-academy-id 16070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087694 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087501 :question/id #uuid "74a9046e-e0a3-59d6-8b13-437e9a0cf04a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4370"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34fd903a-fa34-57b0-a098-08fa1cbd4f95" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348064}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"} :step/id #uuid "17d76850-618a-5b25-88fc-fc5296c2deb2" :step/math-academy-id 16073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088891 :tutorial/content "Some matrices have only one column, for instance\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **column vectors**.\n\nOther matrices have only one row, for instance\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHere, $D$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $E$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix, and $F$ is a $1\\times 4$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **row vectors**.\n\n**Note:** Every number can be thought of as a $1\\times 1$ matrix. For instance, we can consider the number $5$ as the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\,5\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1628e679-983a-5be7-9af6-81f0f45fa29a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2984 :tutorial/title "Matrices With One Row or Column"} :step/id #uuid "1f0e819b-30a9-5921-8910-450a2910edb7" :step/math-academy-id 16114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088898 :tutorial/content "A **square matrix** is a matrix where the number of rows is equal to the number of columns.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a square matrix because it has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. Its dimensions are $2 \\times 2.$\n\nIn general, the dimensions of a square matrix are $n \\times n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "06286713-0de9-5c18-959f-04de81bee510" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3115 :tutorial/title "Square Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "15b361ac-babf-50db-b55f-4a4ad993eb69" :step/math-academy-id 16855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087511 :question/id #uuid "1fcce090-8441-5547-ab52-18b0ff41ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5017"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403328}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"} :step/id #uuid "b0393a9e-8102-5d2d-8b20-ac2709992291" :step/math-academy-id 18438 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088984 :tutorial/content "Matrices arise naturally in *many* real-world problems. Weather forecasting, image processing and computer graphics, video games, and online customization algorithms are just a few of the many applications of matrices. \n\nLet's consider an example where we can use a matrix to model bus routes in a city. \n\nThe diagram below shows the bus routes between three stations (Station A, Station B, and Station C) in a particular city, where $A, B,$ and $C$ represent the stations and the arrows represent the directed routes between them. \n\n![](images/cb/cbdf5c2470229ae3ba10ee18bccd2dc764407897397466b157474bf2b819f5d9.png)\n\nNote that the circles $A,B,$ and $C$ in our diagram are called **nodes**.\n\nWe wish to represent this same diagram using a matrix. Let's start by putting this information into a table like the one below:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** |  |  |  |\n| **B** |  |  |  |  |\n| **C** |  |  |  |  |\n\nLet's now fill in the rows of our table. We begin by considering all of the bus routes from Station A to the other stations:\n\n- There are $\\color{blue}0$ bus routes from $A$ to itself.\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $C.$\n\nFilling in the first row of the table, we get the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\color{blue} 0$ | $\\color{blue} 1$ | $\\color{blue} 1$ |\n| **B** |  |  |  |  |\n| **C** |  |  |  |  |\n\nFilling in the remaining rows in the same way, we get:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, keeping only the essential information, we obtain the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "523d25fd-6b49-5cd7-ac05-80ac6fa0f3d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6114 :tutorial/title "Using Matrices for Modeling"} :step/id #uuid "f1c6aa85-8a73-5586-afeb-0d6c77ff0b9a" :step/math-academy-id 37144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299760}] :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"} :step/id #uuid "e1afb939-c40e-55de-8823-355f35294017" :step/math-academy-id 37441}] :activity/title "Introduction to Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087928}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069850} :activity/id #uuid "f7fe334b-af06-545b-8320-d04ce420b4ed" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049179 :topic/id #uuid "e14dc884-2cd6-5b93-999d-54098588a4da" :topic/math-academy-id 434} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069975 :tutorial/content "When considering the intersection of a line and a reciprocal function, three possibilities can occur.\n\nThe first possibility is that there are two intersection points, as illustrated in the example below.\n\n![](images/00/00f19933ef5f2e7879012dbd9b87fd86964ed44b74e54749dccd2186e02e2a6a.png)\n\nThe second possibility is that there is only one intersection point. For example:\n\n![](images/aa/aabf0d5ea18c01842890b6e470d7c8eaaf36371565ff7c271ea2ab24ccfc9565.png)\n\nThe final possibility is that there are no intersection points. For example:\n\n![](images/da/da7ecba1887505f162144ab3b86e4cca9be104cda7e78ba8fef3d02f10de2abc.png)\n\nTypically, when computing the points of intersection of a reciprocal function and a line, we have to solve a system of two equations. In the first graph that was shown above, this system was\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system of equations can (usually) be transformed into a single rational equation, which can itself be converted into a quadratic equation. For the above system, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{x} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nBy solving the quadratic equation, we can find the intersection points. And like quadratic equations, there can be two, one, or no real solutions. In this case, there are two solutions:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = \\pm 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "70a19ea7-8a66-5b98-b7a2-2312cfe9cf44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3403 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b6e2fa7-06cd-5725-9235-7ae5c5dbd6a0" :step/math-academy-id 466045 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068602 :question/id #uuid "ffb2b712-52b9-5a09-9ac4-10a88f72f57e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5193"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292932}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "674f9cf9-a5de-5045-b6cb-e0e31e295966" :step/math-academy-id 466046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069983 :tutorial/content "Let's now turn our attention to the cases where a line and a reciprocal function intersect at either one point or no points.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find where the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+1$ and the line $y=2$ intersect. To do this, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+1 & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the corresponding $y$-coordinate, we plug $x=1$ into either equation. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$.\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7da7ea3f7e2444197a7bc402309c22fe23d95483909d3d6674645101f036370.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "68835670-e15c-543e-9f1c-ba5dfb7c1823" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4596 :tutorial/title "Intersections of Lines and Reciprocal Functions at One or No Points"} :step/id #uuid "999d3467-9a05-5d20-9fd5-1b8783ff67e3" :step/math-academy-id 466050 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069853}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068601 :question/id #uuid "7fb3c0cb-147c-5d02-a470-c86591394f2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338788}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "663d62ae-d9e6-5064-8a5b-bec6133beefb" :step/math-academy-id 466051 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068606 :question/id #uuid "b7b1d18b-256a-5aa4-9bcb-bd62e512231d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5260"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294908}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"} :step/id #uuid "c86492a6-57ef-5081-b122-627fc0b573d2" :step/math-academy-id 466054}] :activity/title "Finding Intersections of Lines and Reciprocal Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068965}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060686} :activity/id #uuid "ccb8f334-2616-5c6c-bf9f-ac7f7a6bbbf5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422 :topic/id #uuid "f3a09ebe-2d8e-5a69-8681-eb398a98c3dd" :topic/math-academy-id 151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060941 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of linear equations and its respective **augmented matrix**:\n![](images/2c/2c5dfe046037750f98f773ad906094f5ffbad3c285adf200134a119f72888ac8.svg)\n\nIf we can convert the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form, then we can solve the system using back substitution.\n\nThe procedure for reducing $M$ to row echelon form using elementary row operations is called **Gaussian elimination**. To perform Gaussian elimination on a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we use the following procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Consider the entry $a_{11}=2$ of the coefficient matrix. This entry is called a **pivot element**. We want to make this entry equal to $1$. So, the first elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Consider the entry $a_{21}=3$ below the first pivot element. We want to make this entry equal to $0$. So, the second elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=R_2+(-3)R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, consider the entry $a_{22}=-10$. This entry is the second pivot element, and we would like to make it equal to $1$. So, the third elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=\\dfrac{1}{-10}R_2\n$$\n\nand we get $M$ in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{-10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n![](images/b8/b83c4646349bada803311e5e9e160aafc8f08e6f51c7b69302def5ca4eeaa3fa.svg)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3 \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = -1 \n\t\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\tx=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left< \\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right>.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2112d3af-1fb0-5a70-ab99-59931db99930" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3151 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4af04e11-a9c8-594e-af53-b43d73f0e12d" :step/math-academy-id 17188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059584 :question/id #uuid "0a9e6824-677a-5fae-9867-1701e7d8378f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4697"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a10cafc6-9da7-5ffc-a0c7-686a7638fbd9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388195}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 2x2 Augmented Matrix to REF Using Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "2b9b40ab-3cb7-53b3-bb37-414d8e65412f" :step/math-academy-id 17189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060942 :tutorial/content "The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique. In the previous example, after the first row operation we arrived at \n\n![](images/eb/ebcb9bc5aedf609284436ad1c10e98f9b22d91a382136b5aabfb3f18c736ddae.svg)\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n![](images/53/536245bdc7fc9dab02723d3576adff774dfd9711551f472333c027d83f76c05b.svg)\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee4b134d-2908-5930-86bb-32537e0d8347" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3152 :tutorial/title "The Uniqueness of the Row Echelon Form Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "bafcaf30-9671-5655-95bd-e04dfa8750ef" :step/math-academy-id 17190 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059585 :question/id #uuid "1eea81be-26d7-57dd-a622-d66300de7f4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4698"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07abc1cc-5794-51a6-bad4-40565eae52be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414018}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "381e4e89-5395-58a9-bdf6-3f814147b17f" :step/math-academy-id 17191}] :activity/title "Solving 2x2 Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059884}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084121} :activity/id #uuid "77e2555f-bdb8-5497-9a3c-5271eebf55c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049434 :topic/id #uuid "f515510e-ad31-5972-8142-51eb925f2189" :topic/math-academy-id 705} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084449 :tutorial/content "A **combination** is a selection of objects in which order does *not* matter. Two selections of the same items are always counted as the same selections, even if the items are in a different order.\n\nFor example, suppose we have $5$ books but can only take $3$ of them on a trip. How many combinations are there? In other words, how many ways can we take $3$ of the books?\n\nFirst, recall that the total number of ways of selecting $3$ books from $5$ (where order matters) is given by the permutation ${}_5P_3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the permutation calculation ${}_5P_3$ assumes that order of selection matters, but in our problem order is not important. Regardless of the order in which we choose the books, the chosen books are coming with us on the trip and the rest of the books will be left behind.\n\nSo, we must divide by the number of ways to order the $3$ books that we selected, which is $3!.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{}_{5}{P}_{3}}{3!} & =\\frac{60}{3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{6} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $10$ ways that we can take $3$ of the books with us." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e5b5b6a-e449-5f00-9d3a-db539975a666" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3684 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d37a45bc-ec71-5ab7-9073-0fdd10da2df9" :step/math-academy-id 20574 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084450 :tutorial/content "In general, the number of unique combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\frac{{}_nP_r}{r!} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}.\n$$\n\nThis is pronounced \"en-see-arr.\" The \"C\" stands for \"combination.\" \n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books, taking $r=3$ books with us on the trip. Using the formula, the total number of combinations is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times 3!}{2!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times \\cancel{3!}}{2!\\cancel{3!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation for ${}_nC_r$ is $\\displaystyle {{n}\\choose{r}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e346d4a-6e9b-5361-b1da-2f42cbe10deb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3685 :tutorial/title "The General Formula for Combinations"} :step/id #uuid "a20af0b3-5236-5d21-b614-d269c0d8d5f8" :step/math-academy-id 20575 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083224 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083024 :question/id #uuid "e03fa018-0d27-5038-8c77-cf6bb723f9cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392770}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"} :step/id #uuid "820ae5b8-8732-577b-8c88-d57ca0748449" :step/math-academy-id 20576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083025 :question/id #uuid "e87f2616-514d-5b27-9f41-055c1f32f0cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354517}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"} :step/id #uuid "4ba63686-da5f-569b-b077-a477fd0c78cf" :step/math-academy-id 20577 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084123}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083231 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083031 :question/id #uuid "2c61e1e6-58d0-56dd-a63d-29bd5239add1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320acdc4-d3ca-5bc8-85de-cea9cc2fa9c3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048254}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354323}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations"} :step/id #uuid "f342c7eb-9f91-5760-848a-f6dbf2f1723a" :step/math-academy-id 20706 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083312 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083112 :question/id #uuid "bafe3204-ab4c-5db4-ba21-b979e3090cd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7458"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394627}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"} :step/id #uuid "c0f4dad2-f894-549c-8506-2e7f9a1b96a9" :step/math-academy-id 27678 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084124}}] :activity/title "Combinations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083411}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065218} :activity/id #uuid "4206463b-9991-5aa1-a8f1-1ddfceac3676" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593 :topic/id #uuid "030b6230-d213-52b6-874d-139dc628effc" :topic/math-academy-id 314} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065218 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065401 :tutorial/content "Intuitively, a function $f(x)$ is **continuous** if we can draw $y=f(x)$ without taking our pen off the paper. \n\nWe can give a precise mathematical definition of continuity using limits:\n\n> *A function $f(x)$ is **continuous at the point** $\\boldsymbol{x=c}$ if*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c).\n> $$\n\nIn other words, a function is continuous at a point if the limit at the point is equal to the function value at the point.\n\nTo test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist? In other words, we must check that $f(x)$ is defined at $x=c.$\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist? In other words, we have to check that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to c^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to c^{+}}f(x).$\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nIf the answer to all three questions is yes, then the function is continuous at $x=c.$ If the function is not continuous at $x=c,$ we say that it has a **discontinuity** at $x=c.$\n\nThe diagram below shows a function that's continuous at $x=c.$\n\n![](images/54/54ebdb8faa4b1ece5620b817b813780e8abd5cc89cf858eed4cb9ba03f24a046.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(c)$ exists$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exists $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$ $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nLet's now see how to use these continuity conditions to check for continuity in a piecewise function." :tutorial/id #uuid "4a7b79ea-d89f-5d21-948b-8a735e641995" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6695 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "755b1ece-f8b7-54b2-8c09-f8c4ab79bfba" :step/math-academy-id 40541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065222}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065219 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064316 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063916 :question/id #uuid "2b7ec4d6-260d-5fcd-bde2-cb84052868cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-10705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049040}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275312}] :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "a6c61bd4-670b-5aba-b3d3-f57de24f1e4f" :step/math-academy-id 40542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065220}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065220 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064317 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063917 :question/id #uuid "a9fce66e-f0e3-50a6-a2d9-0407b9d91d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10706"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338892}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"} :step/id #uuid "53f7255d-8a19-5fff-954a-4e51d40ed568" :step/math-academy-id 40545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063918 :question/id #uuid "8f95e034-a0fe-5535-9a25-ce549f29c9d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338889}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "f203cd2d-bb6f-5e30-824f-98ee8ab90684" :step/math-academy-id 40548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065222 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065402 :tutorial/content "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3-x & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n- Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3-x) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- We have that $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1).$ $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ceec35c-4700-5ccf-a376-7a6c1fcd5e94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6746 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b7d6d175-7180-513d-95c8-c96d9df56bce" :step/math-academy-id 40849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065219}}] :activity/title "Defining Continuity at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064410}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088609} :activity/id #uuid "bc935836-c5f6-5bf6-8105-6072d1f0946d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049719 :topic/id #uuid "0df888e0-a91f-54c6-bf91-7bc542e0c3e4" :topic/math-academy-id 867} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088930 :tutorial/content "A **dilation** of scale factor $k$ with the center at the origin is a linear transformation that multiplies the standard basis vectors by $k.$ It is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFor example, let \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n be the matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ This transformation is a dilation of scale factor $2.$\n\nTo visualize the dilation $\\mathbf{T},$ consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/6e/6e38185922e9cad6d3961e7c5e755eabf4c52eafd1cc81a1e81a383b4df5ae82.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =Ti=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2i, \\\\ {j}^{'} & =Tj=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ multiplies vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ by $2.$ The resulting vectors span a larger region, shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc6677c641a2a713e0324db90edfdd86a5ca1c096c0ffd70f1216119f36da645.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "689fa0a7-c69e-5bd3-8a10-31aca7d9871f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3536 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b3147c60-fe22-5922-b00c-9f4e25a56724" :step/math-academy-id 20028 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088931 :tutorial/content "A **reflection in the $x$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo visualize a reflection in the $x$-axis, consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square $\\mathrm{R}$ spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/b9/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in the $y$-axis** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAgain, we can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/49/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f38c3a51-d627-5de8-aa0b-e8e8064003c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3537 :tutorial/title "Reflection in the X and Y Axes"} :step/id #uuid "3318223f-ba60-5669-a52f-d290b463770d" :step/math-academy-id 20033 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088665}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087751 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087567 :question/id #uuid "8559e2bf-8b48-5e26-81c6-0db03d93645a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5451"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050103}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401137}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "06bf3e6f-207a-50aa-b32c-11f8a634e387" :step/math-academy-id 20034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087568 :question/id #uuid "ef17b637-7c4d-5515-99b7-13b1290244f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5452"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "166cd7e4-5568-5769-8bc6-4ffc4ed8b508" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393821}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Dilation Given a Vector and its Image"} :step/id #uuid "6cfaedb9-ffad-54bf-a47e-87c0ea841b5a" :step/math-academy-id 20035 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088956 :tutorial/content "A **reflection in line $y=x$** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/0e/0e37a258ea833a2d490fc34a34af40a5c7a18f7589f107c6d0ed631b331c8f2a.png)\n\nSimilarly, a **reflection in line $y=-x$** is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/4a/4a83673a409fd2e5d33b0b5f743f57af08cfb27c38e312a636179102c2172b04.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b34a369-035e-5610-a337-6169ea364331" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4161 :tutorial/title "Reflection in the Lines y=x and y=-x"} :step/id #uuid "0aa5472d-88af-57aa-8bf3-c32dc3b082a4" :step/math-academy-id 25326 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088611}}] :activity/title "Dilations and Reflections as Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087934}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060842} :activity/id #uuid "e59c648d-c7bd-5c18-91b0-045525d672a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050116 :topic/id #uuid "2e6a75f5-b24d-5cd0-adf3-c09c0b1e5607" :topic/math-academy-id 265} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060991 :tutorial/content "The most important distribution in all probability and statistics is the **standard normal distribution.** This distribution describes the **standard normal random variable**, usually denoted as ![](images/9c/9ca1aa3a1f43f4453e28e65cf838ed686bf4e72c5a78668fb7ff7ca85c4e49f2.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) is a **continuous random variable**. Unlike discrete random variables, normally distributed random variables can take any value in the interval ![](images/ff/fffcf74a2091cb9448aad75b366cee9e5b8b3bd2600a8ceec08e669118adbd5b.svg)\n\n- Continuous random variables are described using a continuous curve called the **probability density function** (or PDF). In the case of ![](images/f0/f048f472450bfb3e1a1e3ef76a02338b0879fc88c37a81615f64641de75f0fef.svg) its PDF is denoted ![](images/30/3019d229913464cf5e93fc43c8cdb1a8e973174023d10ea1d345cfb1335bac27.svg)\n\n- ![](images/c2/c2298a29bae4706e73396095897cad806f08f64b05b57550804266d2bb1d9bfa.svg) has the shape of a so-called **bell curve.** The graph of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0907f70d833fe7ce75ae904036b3cab9644e8fe119f91ccedf840bd7b3be549.png)\n\nThe graph has the following important properties:\n\n- The graph is symmetric about the vertical axis.\n\n- The total area under the curve adds up to ![](images/da/daae2fd3251c4ba175906af980d1c33d4db057c616509ec6a146307d64417228.svg)\n\nTo state that a random variable ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) follows a standard normal distribution, we write \n\n![](images/c1/c1a5b6740351318fb48420ebbed2f09086cd253fb88b802c8f6bf138640d75fa.svg)\n\nIn this notation, ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) and ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) refer to the mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/04/04862e2dc7fc74a4f84dbfc83381f578f58b758ce8a99e0ee864d2182b12dfc2.svg) of ![](images/f0/f048f472450bfb3e1a1e3ef76a02338b0879fc88c37a81615f64641de75f0fef.svg) respectively. We'll learn more about this later." :tutorial/id #uuid "eabe62de-e5a2-5dd2-b523-f2b351036186" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8282 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2326b315-ff12-5e21-a2d8-c85a13857529" :step/math-academy-id 68163 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060992 :tutorial/content "An important concept regarding the standard normal distribution is that we calculate probabilities using *the area under the graph* of $f(z).$\n\nFor example, suppose that the (continuous) random variable $Z$ follows a standard normal distribution:\n\n$$\nZ\\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nHow do we calculate $P(Z\\leq 1)?$\n\nBy definition, this probability is given by the area under the graph of $f(z)$ over the interval $z\\in (-\\infty, 1],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8e3e9ac10771b4433375f323b24c8813d3a3dacb5b67897292f90fd21cd3d88.png)\n\nIt can be shown that this area equals $0.8413$ to four decimal places. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq 1) = 0.8413.\n$$\n\nNote the following important points:\n\n- Since $Z$ is continuous, there is no difference between $P(Z\\leq 1)$ and $P(Z < 1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\lt 1) = 0.8413.\n$$\n\nIt doesn't matter whether $z\\in(-\\infty, 1]$ or $z\\in (-\\infty, 1),$ the area under the graph is the same.\n\n- The probability that $Z$ equals a specific value is zero! For example,\n\n$$\nP(Z = 1) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is because the area under the graph of $f(z)$ at the specific value $z=1$ equals zero.\n\nThe last property makes intuitive sense. Since $Z$ can be *any* value in the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty),$ the probability of reaching any particular value is vanishingly small." :tutorial/id #uuid "c9b15cb5-b5e4-5d86-8489-2e6f16d84981" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8283 :tutorial/title "Calculating Probabilities Using Areas"} :step/id #uuid "91c82fa8-0a73-50ae-ae6f-86c2dee36dc1" :step/math-academy-id 68164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060993 :tutorial/content "The **cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution** is defined by\n\n$$\n\\Phi(z) = P(Z \\leq z),\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z \\leq z)$ denotes the probability that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ is less than some fixed value $z.$ \n\n![](images/43/43b5489abd69bd4bb9558dcb0850bab2e19a9c9794ec093f73c5e163b46bb402.png)\n\nFor example, using the result we saw earlier, \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq 1) = \\Phi(1)  = 0.8413\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8a2c99700ce2c2b7d6808da06c83570da25ee0107ad5d796f637b7dd1079c90c.png)\n\nThe symbol $\\Phi$ is called \"Phi\" and rhymes with \"fly.\"\n\nWhen we need to find values of $\\Phi(z),$ we often use a lookup table, like the one shown below:\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.504 \\,$ | $\\, 0.508 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5438 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5478 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5832 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5871 \\,$ |\n\nThe table above represents the following values of the cumulative distribution:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\Phi(0) &= 0.5 & \\Phi(0.01) &= 0.504 & \\Phi(0.02) &= 0.508 \\\\[3pt]\n\\Phi(0.10) &= 0.5398 & \\Phi(0.11) &= 0.5438 & \\Phi(0.12) &= 0.5478 \\\\[3pt]\n\\Phi(0.20) &= 0.5793 & \\Phi(0.21) &= 0.5832 & \\Phi(0.22) &= 0.5871 \n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFor example, to determine the value of $\\Phi({\\color{red}0.12})$ from the table, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Select the row that corresponds to ${\\color{red}0.1}$ and the column that corresponds to $0.0{\\color{red}2}.$\n\n- Take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.504 \\,$ | $\\, 0.508 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5438 \\,$ | $\\color{blue}\\, 0.5478 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5832 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5871 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq 0.12) = \\Phi(0.12)=0.5478.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since the function $f(z)$ is symmetric about the vertical axis and the total area under the curve must equal $1,$ we must have $\\Phi(0) = 0.5.$ This value is shown in the table." :tutorial/id #uuid "5480f560-2b25-5d75-bdf2-c8fc0552343f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8284 :tutorial/title "The Standard Normal Cumulative Distribution Function"} :step/id #uuid "f1ec77cf-8895-5db7-9815-6ee6de5ab59b" :step/math-academy-id 68165 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059426 :question/id #uuid "7fcfa422-4cc0-5798-8bc3-4824de1a3826" :question/math-academy-id "e-13266"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77015ca5-d392-500e-93dc-8e065dd1c06c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050256}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357655}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "ffdc481b-b079-5af8-996f-33730204b27a" :step/math-academy-id 68166 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059427 :question/id #uuid "a376a597-d6ab-5d51-8c12-196ed793150d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13267"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e376bffa-02ae-5575-af2c-4ad5e703f9fc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357675}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "a7388a8c-899a-5d2d-9cd7-55398fd4e106" :step/math-academy-id 68169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059428 :question/id #uuid "d95c5015-9dc4-50a5-85bc-50bab9bf0650" :question/math-academy-id "e-13268"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "607e0800-870c-5271-9220-0489d9c5b891" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357688}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "bce2670c-5f88-568b-ae1b-56ec6cc899e7" :step/math-academy-id 68172}] :activity/title "The Standard Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059940}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079565} :activity/id #uuid "f8a08871-6a9b-51b3-ac32-da28357d0c46" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050215 :topic/id #uuid "36fbe9c5-6e9c-5bcb-84f4-f05b614b557b" :topic/math-academy-id 626} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079716 :tutorial/content "The additive properties of integrals are beneficial when we have to integrate a piecewise function, such as $$f(x)= {\\begin{aligned}x, & x<0 \\\\ 5, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor example, let's calculate the integral $$\\displaystyle \\int_{-5}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$$\n\nWe can split the integral into two integrals using the additive property, one integral for each \"piece\" of the piecewise function.\n\n- The first integral will be over the integral $[-5,0],$ and\n- the second integral will be over the integral $[0,4].$\n\nDoing this, we get the following: $$\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-5}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{-5}^{0}+5x{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}[{0}^{2}-(-5)^{2}]+5[4-0] \\\\ & =-\\frac{25}{2}+20 \\\\ & =\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3ee5b005-6d82-503b-a69b-0cb98d633bc6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 805 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c73cec04-a925-5876-81c3-89b6c2a0e2ff" :step/math-academy-id 613819 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078597 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078309 :question/id #uuid "df884ed9-c75e-5a38-abe2-425e01ee4aa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-432"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377360}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"} :step/id #uuid "b7a0b578-f622-52c1-b4fb-081198c177db" :step/math-academy-id 613820 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078310 :question/id #uuid "317454f0-3a04-5c1c-a451-2b8b2ec850ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-433"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048227}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377276}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "23430d31-1956-5ee7-b21d-9a80dee9d166" :step/math-academy-id 613823 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079568}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078311 :question/id #uuid "189d4a9a-fb2c-57b2-b9f6-9bec14fa1062" :question/math-academy-id "e-434"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377568}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "379a1a51-878e-5d9a-a00f-3acf7c8e6103" :step/math-academy-id 613826}] :activity/title "Definite Integrals of Piecewise Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078652}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055867} :activity/id #uuid "542d0295-d41f-5fa8-9378-f60eaae6241d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050254 :topic/id #uuid "39e988d3-6f10-5718-8fbd-1ad04a159a54" :topic/math-academy-id 48} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056041 :tutorial/content "Recall that a set is **finite** if it contains a finite number of elements, while a set is **infinite** if it contains infinitely many elements.\n\nThe **cardinality** of a *finite* set $S,$ denoted $|S|,$ is the number of elements in $S.$\n\nFor example: \n\n- The set $A,$ given by\n$A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$\ncontains $3$ elements:\n\n$$\na, \\quad b, \\quad c\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality is $|A|=3$\n\n- The set $B,$ given by\n$B = \\{2, 4, \\{ 6, 8\\}, \\emptyset\\}$\ncontains $4$ elements:\n\n$$\n2, \\quad 4, \\quad \\{6,8\\}, \\quad \\emptyset\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality is $|B| = 4.$\n\n- The set $C,$ given by\n$C = \\{ 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 8, 9 \\}$ \ncontains $5$ *unique* elements:\n\n$$\n5, \\quad 6, \\quad 7, \\quad 8, \\quad 9\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality $|C| = 5.$\n\nThe cardinality of a set is sometimes called its **cardinal number.** So, the cardinal numbers of $A,B,$ and $C$ are $3,4,$ and $5,$ respectively.\n\nNote that the empty set \n\n$$\n\\emptyset = \\{\\}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $\\left|\\emptyset\\right| = 0$ since it contains no elements. The empty set is the *only* set with cardinality zero.\n\nFinally, you might be wondering about the cardinality of infinite sets. For example, what is the cardinality of the set of natural numbers?\n\n$$\n|\\mathbb N| = |\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}| = \\,?\n$$\n\nWhen a set is infinite, we do *not* usually write $|\\mathbb N| = \\infty.$ The reason is that although $\\mathbb N$ is infinite, so is $\\mathbb R,$ and there is a sense in which $|\\mathbb R|$ is \"larger than\" $|\\mathbb N|.$ Thus, we need an entirely new system for labeling cardinalities of infinite sets. These ideas will be discussed at length in future lessons. \n\nWe'll consider finite sets only for the remainder of this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0648f3b-bec4-5689-ac23-ac70496ecab0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8218 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6f256f93-5254-5f5e-be85-a9cc0aaec093" :step/math-academy-id 65851 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054433 :question/id #uuid "cff493c6-48c0-502b-9306-9f7bc262099d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb35fcff-d9f0-5ae8-999e-7e8bbc0b0919" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413067}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Finite Set"} :step/id #uuid "b33646a0-dc4c-5cfc-ad0b-dff69f463110" :step/math-academy-id 65852 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054434 :question/id #uuid "3dd5ad9c-c449-5072-aebb-d8980dcc0ef1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24ce056e-1fe1-55d4-b88c-62629435003b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413063}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Set With Set Elements"} :step/id #uuid "4a48db97-681b-5f5d-bb68-1b28f464bb36" :step/math-academy-id 65856 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054439 :question/id #uuid "e794793e-f22b-5072-be07-8b77d29d3b22" :question/math-academy-id "e-13169"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049803}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413065}] :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"} :step/id #uuid "e25d6464-061e-502f-adc5-8c7954ff5743" :step/math-academy-id 65860 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055871}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054440 :question/id #uuid "1acb903b-bdf0-5d16-8a1c-6fdce21f4c84" :question/math-academy-id "e-13172"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413068}] :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"} :step/id #uuid "dd47968e-7e76-5958-82e5-464e79fbb5cd" :step/math-academy-id 65863}] :activity/title "Cardinality of Finite Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054907}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084086} :activity/id #uuid "705783c6-92f8-5756-875f-7776d0636e4e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050379 :topic/id #uuid "445c7063-c967-5f21-b424-ab8820e310ca" :topic/math-academy-id 759} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084529 :tutorial/content "An **improper integral of the second kind** is the integral of the form \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{\\color{red}{a}}^{\\color{blue}{b}} f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere the integration limits $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are both finite, and the function $f(x)$ is unbounded as we approach one of these limits.\n\nFor instance, the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\color{red}{0}}^{\\color{blue}{1}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nis an improper integral of the second kind because \n\n- both limits ($\\color{red}0$ and $\\color{blue}1$) are finite, and\n\n- the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach one of the limits (the *lower* limit in this case):\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{red}0}^+\n$$\n\nLet's look at some examples of integrals that are *not* improper integrals of the second kind:\n\n- For the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nthe upper limit is infinite. Therefore, it is *not* an improper integral of the second kind.\n\n- For the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\color{red}{1}}^{\\color{blue}{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nthe function $f(x)$ under the integral is finite at both limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1\\ne \\pm \\infty  \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ne \\pm \\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is *not* an improper integral of the second kind." :tutorial/id #uuid "914178e3-0083-53cf-b696-117a81c11b7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 936 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b87a4ade-551c-5bd9-9950-2d4f5d1d6118" :step/math-academy-id 1834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083202 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083006 :question/id #uuid "70734fff-e45d-5b6d-9643-9e94dbcd2fcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351456}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind"} :step/id #uuid "43059783-227c-5701-896c-3c23540fc535" :step/math-academy-id 1835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083103 :question/id #uuid "8853c8c3-821c-5ae5-9817-ae8e6d7a55a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-7297"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332788}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"} :step/id #uuid "85990fb3-a980-51b6-9f9f-7ba77dd799df" :step/math-academy-id 26996 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083343 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083146 :question/id #uuid "201e4ce6-d718-5cd1-ba7a-851accf314ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-9959"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351451}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting One of the Limits of an Improper Integral"} :step/id #uuid "545fcfaa-4092-5192-8d45-5e7f8f8084ed" :step/math-academy-id 37504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084493 :tutorial/content "Let's once again consider the following improper integral:\n\n$$\nI = \\int_{\\color{red}{0}}^{\\color{blue}1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThe function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in ({\\color{red}{0}},{\\color{blue}{1}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{red}0}^+\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a, 1]$ for some parameter $a\\in  ({\\color{red}{0}},{\\color{blue}{1}})$ and taking the limit as $a\\to{\\color{red}0}^+\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nI = \\lim_{a\\to{\\color{red}0}^+} \\int_a^1 \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this integral, we follow three steps.\n\n* **Step 1:** First, we identify the endpoint at which [math]f(x)[/math] becomes unbounded. \n\n* **Step 2:** Next, we set up a one-sided limit that approaches the unbounded endpoint from the interior of the integration domain:\n\n  * If the function is unbounded at the lower endpoint, we approach it from the right-hand side.\n\n  * Alternatively, if the function is unbounded at the upper endpoint, we approach it from the left-hand side.\n\n* **Step 3:** Finally, we evaluate the limit.\n\nIn our example above, the unbound endpoint is the lower one, [math]a=0.[/math] \n\nHence, we set up the limit as the lower endpoint approaches the value [math]a=0[/math] from the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^1\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{a\\rightarrow 0^+}\\left(\\int_a^1\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n\\]\n\nEvaluating the integral and the limit, we get\n\n\\begin{align}\n\\lim_{a\\rightarrow 0^+}\\left(\\int_a^1\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n\t&= \\lim_{a\\rightarrow 0^+}\\left(\\int_a^1x^{-{1}/{2}}\\textrm{d}x\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\lim_{a\\rightarrow 0^+}\\left([2x^{{1}/{2}}]_a^1\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\lim_{a\\rightarrow 0^+}\\left([2\\sqrt{1}] - [2\\sqrt{a}]\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t&= \\lim_{a\\rightarrow 0^+}\\left(2 - 2\\sqrt{a}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\t&= 2.\n\\end{align}\n\nSo, the integral converged to a finite value even though [math]f(x)[/math] is unbounded at the lower endpoint." :tutorial/id #uuid "11646955-da2c-56e3-b0e3-56750dbbc067" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6188 :tutorial/title "Improper Integrals of the Second Kind: Rewriting the Lower Limit"} :step/id #uuid "14ec9a20-9280-5b3a-9a8f-6ec429a8dd08" :step/math-academy-id 37505 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084494 :tutorial/content "Now let's consider the following improper integral:\n\n$$\nI = \\int_{\\color{red}0}^{\\color{blue}{2}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x-2}}\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThe function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in ({\\color{red}{0}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x-2}} \\rightarrow -\\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}2}^-\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0, b]$ for some parameter $b\\in ({\\color{red}{0}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ and taking the limit as $b\\to{\\color{blue}2}^-\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nI = \\lim_{b\\to{\\color{blue}2}^-} \\int_{0}^b \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x-2}}\\textrm{d}x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c11dfa32-a84e-53b5-903e-b3d1a98203d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6243 :tutorial/title "Improper Integrals of the Second Kind: Rewriting the Upper Limit"} :step/id #uuid "aa5a56fa-de77-50ff-a12c-23538d135e27" :step/math-academy-id 37875 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084495 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to construct improper integrals of the second kind. Now, let's learn how to evaluate them.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\frac{\\textrm d x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function is unbounded at the upper limit of integration:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad x\\rightarrow 1^-\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to evaluate the integral by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $b,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $b\\to 1^-.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to {1}^{-}}{\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}x{|}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to {1}^{-}}[\\operatorname{arcsin}b-\\operatorname{arcsin}0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to {1}^{-}}[\\operatorname{arcsin}b-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}b \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\frac{\\textrm d x}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the limit is infinite or does not exist, we say that the integral is **divergent**. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "43dd7306-44f4-51f6-85d1-14c61a5678df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6244 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"} :step/id #uuid "f3cc3aa5-52bc-554b-9737-6604afca254f" :step/math-academy-id 37881 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084087}}] :activity/title "Improper Integrals of the Second Kind" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083449}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074759} :activity/id #uuid "f6ae1858-5ee1-5d31-814d-c2d09d20e0b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050409 :topic/id #uuid "46beb5e4-992f-546d-8051-796c949740a1" :topic/math-academy-id 526} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075026 :tutorial/content "The shortest distance between a point and a line is the length of the line segment that connects the point to the line and is perpendicular to the line.\n\nFor example, consider the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/06/06444505f504ecd01a91d4846b46bf202caafa9d9f756958483262f69b5db14b.svg) shown below:\n\n![](images/8c/8cba6df956e809981ac4a3ae78a6f6843be0ca8561fbcadd9d9ba98f2a5325cc.png)\n\nTo find the distance between the point and the line, we construct a segment with its endpoints on the point and the line, and which is perpendicular to the line\n\n![](images/80/802b30a788850457206716430755da2d5e3c6b3a53633c95626e49ee668d2507.png)\n\nThe distance between the point and the line is the length of this segment, which we compute as the absolute value of the difference in ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the point and the line:\n\n![](images/11/11e3682c2b811da299d9c475bd0ba3684d0c7f68e5677d925744b37026c47b0c.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1d8ad60c-5b38-530d-abc1-d03020dca8b2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7819 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "be614ac7-a2e7-5e60-8bca-ad9de9ca9731" :step/math-academy-id 45229 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073434 :question/id #uuid "2ce03c34-8e2c-5554-a129-1eaf05ad46fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342183}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "9f8070e4-2aac-5a71-be57-98f43a5de8dc" :step/math-academy-id 45230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075027 :tutorial/content "Consider the line $l$ with the equation $y=-x + 6$ and the point $Q(2,1)$ that does not lie on $l.$\n\n![](images/83/8353825890413432e74d30512219cb95edc76fe8c24e7a824d6893c745c48bd1.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find the point $P$ that lies on $l$ that's closest to the point $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/f8/f880cfde6bdfe07c6804609f9b052189632afcde4907207de5245298aea9ea6e.png)\n\nNow, here's the key idea: $P$ is the point that lies on $l$ *and* lies on the line *perpendicular* to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\nSo, to find $P,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the line perpendicular to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\n- Then, we find the intersection of the two lines.\n\nWe know that one endpoint of the segment is [math](2,1).[/math] To find the other endpoint, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to [math]y=-x+6[/math] and passes through the point [math](2,1),[/math] and then find where it intersects [math]y=-x+6.[/math]\n\nThe line $y=-x + 6$ has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1$ (the negative reciprocal). It also must pass through the point $(2,1).$ So, using point-slope form, we find that the equation of the perpendicular line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =x-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the lines $y=-x+6$ and $y=x-1$ intersect, we set the equations equal to each other and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+6 & =x-1 \\\\ 6+1 & =x+x \\\\ 7 & =2x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ So, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( \\dfrac{7}{2}, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8fd7f6d4-4f53-50f8-9d72-cceb3d3315ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7820 :tutorial/title "Finding the Point on a Line Closest to Another Point"} :step/id #uuid "e4482ee1-af85-5a92-b2a1-929166abc8b4" :step/math-academy-id 45233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073435 :question/id #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12549"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "37db8424-1ef2-55d0-b76f-cd55f5ac5469" :step/math-academy-id 45234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073436 :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "14381924-124a-5e92-85f9-6c4bc617cf97" :step/math-academy-id 45522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074768}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075028 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we found that the point $P\\left(\\dfrac72,\\dfrac52\\right)$ lies on the line $y=-x+6$ and is the closest point on this line to the point $Q(2,1).$\n\n![](images/11/11901390427d5cd79af514a589175044cafebf47becdae84045005f4564de090.png)\n\nTo calculate the shortest *distance* between $Q$ and the line, we compute the distance between $P$ and $Q$ using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2}-2)}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shortest distance between the line $y=-x+6$ and $Q(2,1)$ is $\\dfrac{ 3 }{ \\sqrt{2} } .$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb4d84ad-bfc1-5e9f-9da6-1447abdc11bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7916 :tutorial/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line"} :step/id #uuid "a2673e24-58fd-5d2a-98b3-03e91da98a5b" :step/math-academy-id 45600 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074762}}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073946}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055562} :activity/id #uuid "87e8f158-746a-5649-b184-0df39a6a3af1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432 :topic/id #uuid "48930849-af42-52a8-8e89-28eb54e1d4a5" :topic/math-academy-id 49} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056042 :tutorial/content "An **ordered pair** $(x, y)$ is a pair of mathematical objects $x$ and $y$ written in parentheses and separated by a comma. \n\nYou are already familiar with an ordered pair $(x,y)$ representing a point in the Cartesian plane. In this context, $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. However, more generally, the coordinates $x$ and $y$ of an ordered pair can be any mathematical objects we like! Moreover, they do not need to be the same type of object. \n\nHere are some examples of ordered pairs.\n\n![](images/bf/bf99b45f9e9ab363314f3604ef6316c7fe7e63623452f2fe78d597dd60c2d302.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We say that an ordered pair $(x,y)$ has **coordinates** $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Given two ordered pairs $(a, b)$ and $(c, d),$ we say they're **equal** if $a = c$ and $b = d,$ and we write \n\n$$\n(a, b) = (c, d).\n$$\n\nFor example, $(2, 3) = (2, 3)$ but $(2, 3) \\ne (3, 2).$\n\nWe use ordered pairs to define the so-called **Cartesian product** of two sets. Let's discuss this in more detail." :tutorial/id #uuid "81aaf185-55f8-5a2a-835d-f54af39febdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8219 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0df08950-a832-5cd0-b63b-78118f28d77e" :step/math-academy-id 152907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056052 :tutorial/content "The **Cartesian product** of two sets $A$ and $B,$ denoted $A \\times B,$ is a set containing all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$  \n\nWe define the Cartesian product using the following constructive definition:\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the following finite sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{x, y\\}, \\qquad B  = \\{1, 2, 3\\}\n$$\n\nTo help visualize the Cartesian product $A\\times B,$ we'll use a visual schematic.\n\nFirst, we create a table and list the elements of $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](images/ec/ec3087067a79d46751e7ae67d34543b0507f3425e92a50eb8fb242b35b463b8b.png)\n\nThen, at each location marked $\\ast$, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and whose second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\n![](images/f9/f91a0e2ec091f90395c59330597b55c9e55aad73ac4bc052e2b500ea9db894b2.png)\n\nTo write down the Cartesian product $A\\times B,$ we construct a set containing all ordered pairs in our schematic. This gives\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(x, 1), (x, 2), (x, 3), (y, 1), (y, 2), (y, 3)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's now consider $B\\times A.$ The schematic for this set is shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a59aca653639bf7499634ade12390f0c4d0f6fb0bb3dd34359fc5a53c49da3d8.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB\\times A = \\big\\{(1, x), (1, y), (2, x), (2, y), (3, x), (3, y)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $(a,b)\\neq (b,a),$ we have that $A\\times B  \\neq B\\times A$ in general. \n\nWe can also construct the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$ The schematic for this set is given below. \n\n![](images/00/00008245f5a72373c268ebe551e707b0f981c7ee03ec3cb0351288ea9d625f03.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA\\times A = A^2 = \\big\\{(x, x), (x, y), (y, x), (y, y)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that the Cartesian product of *any* set with the empty set equals the empty set:\n\n$$\nA \\times \\emptyset = \\emptyset, \\qquad \\emptyset \\times A = \\emptyset\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "836707d4-4237-574f-8d5b-11cc0fc8fcd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8306 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Product"} :step/id #uuid "ebc60697-b06e-52cf-8c5c-585f4265afbf" :step/math-academy-id 152908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054633 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054438 :question/id #uuid "2bc77b3d-fca6-5f66-ac12-e9fedcce15bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-13160"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421483}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"} :step/id #uuid "59d53061-c8fe-5aad-9e2b-1bda55f89078" :step/math-academy-id 152909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054435 :question/id #uuid "4f2ec8a1-0ab4-55ea-9472-2dd7b9fa5f8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13151"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420986}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"} :step/id #uuid "4923937f-f045-5632-9168-4a3187eb7f4d" :step/math-academy-id 152912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056043 :tutorial/content "Consider the following two sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{x,y\\}, \\qquad B = \\{1,2,3\\}\n$$\n\nWhat is the cardinality of $A\\times B?$ In other words, how many elements are contained in this set?\n\nLet's start by visualizing our $A\\times B$ schematic:\n\n![](images/86/8626e58fdf53aca4411c4656326f4ff6cbcb1deec0a58870b06e0f7c05c39e19.png)\n\nNotice that our schematic has\n\n- $2$ columns, corresponding to the number of elements in $A$ (i.e., $|A|=2$), and\n\n- $3$ rows, corresponding to the number of elements in $B$ (i.e., $|B|=3$).\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n|A\\times B| = |A|\\cdot|B| = 2\\cdot 3 = 6.\n$$\n\nIn general, if $A$ and $B$ are *finite* sets, then\n\n$$\n| A \\times B | = | A | \\cdot | B |\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4968f90a-cf92-5a2e-9762-652f1c7081ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8220 :tutorial/title "The Cardinality of a Cartesian Product"} :step/id #uuid "96da29ee-e81b-575e-b280-c467fafb16fd" :step/math-academy-id 152915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054635 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054437 :question/id #uuid "677fc67f-94df-57a7-a804-22337acda702" :question/math-academy-id "e-13154"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88fb08f1-03f1-5b04-8b48-4b153e522040" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050254}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420988}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cardinality of a Cartesian Product"} :step/id #uuid "649940bd-db05-5312-a0f4-ee26e6d066ec" :step/math-academy-id 152916 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055568}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056053 :tutorial/content "We can extend the definition of the Cartesian product to include three or more sets.\n\nFor example, if we have three sets $A,B,$ and $C,$ then the Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ is defined as\n\n$$\nA\\times B\\times C = \\big\\{(a, b, c) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B, c\\in C \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, $A\\times B\\times C$ contains all **ordered triples** $(a,b,c)$ such that $a$ belongs to $A, b$ belongs to $B,$ and $c$ belongs to $C.$\n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\nA=\\{{\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\quad B=\\{{\\color{red}{x}}, {\\color{red}{y}}\\},\\quad \\quad C=\\{z\\},\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nA\\times B\\times C = \\Big\\{({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{x}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{y}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{red}{x}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{red}{y}},z)\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can extend the cardinality rule as follows:\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times C| = |A|\\cdot|B|\\cdot |C|\n$$\n\nSo, in the case above,\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times C| = |A|\\cdot|B|\\cdot |C| = 2\\cdot2\\cdot 1 = 4.\n$$\n\nNothing stops us from defining the Cartesian product of any finite number of sets! So, if $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$ denotes a collection of $n$ sets, then\n\n$$\nA_1\\times A_2\\times\\cdots\\times A_n =  \\Big\\{(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n) \\: : \\: a_i \\in A_i, 1\\leq i\\leq n \\Big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $(a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_n)$ is an **ordered tuple**. \n\nOnce again, the cardinality rule extends intuitively:\n\n$$\n|A_1\\times A_2\\times \\cdots \\times A_n| = |A_1|\\cdot|A_2|\\cdots |A_n|\n$$\n\nThe **Cartesian power** $A^n$ of a set $A$ for natural $n$ is defined as\n\n$$\nA^n = \\underbrace{A\\times A\\times\\cdots\\times A}_{n \\textrm{ times}} =  \\Big\\{(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n) \\: : \\: a_i \\in A, 1\\leq i\\leq n \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =A\\times A\\times A \\\\  & ={1,2}\\times {1,2}\\times {1,2} \\\\  & ={(1,1,1)\\,(1,1,2)\\,(1,2,1)\\,(1,2,2)\\,(2,1,1)\\,(2,1,2)\\,(2,2,1)\\,(2,2,2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e7babb8f-03f0-5758-abb5-25fc9b729fa0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8312 :tutorial/title "Generalizing the Cartesian Product"} :step/id #uuid "51df1969-1eaf-580f-bc49-560a258104c7" :step/math-academy-id 152919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056054 :tutorial/content "We must pay special attention whenever parentheses are used in Cartesian products.\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times B\\times C & \\ne A\\times (B\\times C),\\,\\text{and}\\,A\\times B\\times C\\ne (A\\times B)\\times C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we also have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times (B\\times C)\\ne (A\\times B)\\times C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why, note that $A\\times (B\\times C)$ denotes the Cartesian product of $A$ with $B\\times C.$ Expressing it formally using a constructive definition, we have\n\n$$\nA\\times (B\\times C) = \\Big\\{\\left(a, (b, c)\\right) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, (b,c) \\in B\\times C \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, $A\\times (B\\times C)$ contains all ordered *pairs* (not triples) such that the first coordinate $a$ belongs to $A,$ and the second coordinate is an ordered pair $(b,c)$ belonging to $B\\times C.$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times C = \\Big\\{\\left( (a, b), c\\right) \\: : \\: (a, b) \\in A\\times B,\\, c\\in C \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo understand this concretely, let's recall the sets $A,B,$ and $C$ defined earlier\n\n$$\nA=\\{{\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\quad B=\\{{\\color{red}{x}}, {\\color{red}{y}}\\},\\quad \\quad C=\\{z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere we found that\n\n$$\nA\\times B\\times C = \\Big\\{({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{x}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{y}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{red}{x}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{red}{y}},z)\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $A\\times (B\\times C)$ and $(A\\times B)\\times C$ for these sets: \n\n- To calculate $A\\times (B\\times C),$ we first note that\n\n$$\nB\\times C = \\big\\{({\\color{red}{x}},z), ({\\color{red}{y}},z)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nand so the product $A\\times (B\\times C)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times (B\\times C) & ={1,2}\\times {(x,z),(y,z)} \\\\  & ={(1,(x,z)),\\,(1,(y,z)),\\,(2,(x,z)),\\,(2,(y,z))} \\\\  & \\ne A\\times B\\times C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, to calculate $(A\\times B)\\times C,$ we first note that\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \n\\big\\{\n({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{x}}),\\,\n({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{y}}),\\,\n({\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{red}{x}}),\\,\n({\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{red}{y}})\n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nand so the product $A\\times (B\\times C)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A\\times B)\\times C & ={(1,x),\\,(1,y),\\,(2,x),\\,(2,y)}\\times {z} \\\\  & ={((1,x),z),\\,((1,y),z),\\,((2,x),z),\\,((2,y),z)} \\\\  & \\ne A\\times B\\times C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, note that the cardinalities of all three sets are equal:\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times C| = |A\\times (B\\times C)| = |(A\\times B)\\times C|\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d6f63dd9-f884-5cff-9cf5-f942e17bf946" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8314 :tutorial/title "Using Parentheses in Cartesian Products"} :step/id #uuid "fcfd1a47-012b-57d7-a639-6b2f69b3e4ca" :step/math-academy-id 152920 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054436 :question/id #uuid "0a101406-0053-5507-8989-3c1cd6e8d4e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13152"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421484}] :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"} :step/id #uuid "9ef17f54-80f8-5b28-a9ca-bbc0979b6886" :step/math-academy-id 152921}] :activity/title "The Cartesian Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054908}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069567} :activity/id #uuid "09c8fe50-cab2-5dfd-a90a-5053ffffc1bf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574 :topic/id #uuid "54ee2cc0-54a0-5d60-a3d0-0b11995e4133" :topic/math-academy-id 462} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069958 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fb32bea-3d77-5134-936c-bfa9f0381fdb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2583 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3561bec4-880a-5815-bc18-84c3d62a3191" :step/math-academy-id 12020 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068547 :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3567"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392615}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "1c9dde49-f9f9-5036-8e84-b7e1ced3796b" :step/math-academy-id 12021 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068548 :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3568"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396673}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"} :step/id #uuid "74cd2bd0-c1c2-5429-adda-d13aa18601d2" :step/math-academy-id 12022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068549 :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051049}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329857}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"} :step/id #uuid "64c70852-38a7-5f5e-ade8-36856f8eef67" :step/math-academy-id 12023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069961 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "54ca4bef-1b8e-5315-85d4-0667910b332c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2640 :tutorial/title "Continuity at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "c5269250-b2f9-525d-bfaa-9f0e6033687b" :step/math-academy-id 12404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069568}}] :activity/title "Determining Continuity from Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068989}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069571} :activity/id #uuid "acac3ae8-22e4-541d-b0da-b38b7e642d06" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050657 :topic/id #uuid "5cf444d7-d014-59c7-b47c-21b442662b58" :topic/math-academy-id 474} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069959 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **left-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^-} f(x) = f(c).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $(-\\infty,2]$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14b92b0e5a0f482b5e58da975f6f8d369890af97ddc562a4876e41c496f5dfef.png)\n\nNotice that at $x=2,$ the left limit exists, while right limit does not exist because $f(x)$ is not defined for $x>2.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nBecause the right-sided limit does not exist, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$ However, because the left-sided limit exists and is equal to the function value at $x=2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4 = f(2),\n$$\n\nwe can say that the function is **left-continuous** at $x=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "75d31b95-b022-5133-8b0d-54a5ba38c74f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2596 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "27206838-7dfd-50f0-a4b5-a92905b05017" :step/math-academy-id 12117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068550 :question/id #uuid "0acc185c-cc26-523f-981a-3e024f0693a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050454}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341902}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b43b6d85-ec3f-5921-878b-59ca9e5c066f" :step/math-academy-id 12118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069960 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **right-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^+} f(x) = f(c).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[-2,\\infty)$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b625f271c8889466c2a19ed8a333b05e70c2e852058cc1d4469f428d761c799.png)\n\nThe function is **right-continuous** at $x=-2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2^+}f(x) = 4 = f(-2).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9424b0a9-43f3-5c35-b8b9-b78b9c69e816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2597 :tutorial/title "Right Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "55c60e5f-cbe7-5688-8af0-b8fa5d2c54d1" :step/math-academy-id 12119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068551 :question/id #uuid "e9a8cbce-3bed-567b-9e0e-e1382465a681" :question/math-academy-id "e-3590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341907}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "b88e81b2-e4ca-5ffc-8ade-1a44b0fecea1" :step/math-academy-id 12120 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068552 :question/id #uuid "1ec6e675-6052-5cec-8b29-766084245f74" :question/math-academy-id "e-3591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341909}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "1017d5f3-a1be-5979-aa84-1860d0b66f77" :step/math-academy-id 12121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069962 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is said to be **continuous** at a point $x=c$ if \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c) .\n$$\n\nIn order for the above statement to be true, we require both\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {c}^{-}}f(x)=f(c)\\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to {c}^{+}}f(x)=f(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equalities represent left-continuity and right-continuity. So, we have the following fact:\n\n> A function is **continuous** at a point if and only if it is *both* **left-continuous** *and* **right-continuous** at that point." :tutorial/id #uuid "1dd8ea3c-49f9-5c85-98b3-85b0542e7a8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2641 :tutorial/title "Defining Continuity in Terms of Left-Continuity and Right-Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "c70c04f3-5555-555a-93b3-8518806961e9" :step/math-academy-id 12456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069575}}] :activity/title "Left and Right Continuity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068998}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060675} :activity/id #uuid "5052a9d6-d9c5-5df7-993c-818d53b162fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765 :topic/id #uuid "64dbabd7-08e6-5435-b006-ab4997312f47" :topic/math-academy-id 148} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060938 :tutorial/content "We can use matrices to solve systems of linear equations. To do this, we need to set up the **augmented matrix** for the system.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=-1 \\\\ -3x+7y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the **coefficient matrix**, which consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we create the **augmented matrix**. This is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the future, we will learn how to use coefficient and augmented matrices to solve systems of equations. But for now, let's practice setting them up." :tutorial/id #uuid "36b1a271-77b9-59ed-9f12-1b6675c26830" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3146 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5b2031d1-6747-5c9a-b8e2-711f6dd1e094" :step/math-academy-id 17169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059767 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059576 :question/id #uuid "b2196eb1-6691-549b-950a-a9bab6863a70" :question/math-academy-id "e-4683"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb13e9d9-19f0-5f39-b9f8-e6e2f8fac433" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338975}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "8e2bc116-2181-5120-9389-ff1ba9b3ee5e" :step/math-academy-id 17170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059577 :question/id #uuid "b5967dd6-bdf9-5cd0-9639-ec2816d04256" :question/math-academy-id "e-4684"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9ccaa5-d69c-584d-946e-0c57b3050160" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338976}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "e6584e81-18d3-5c82-b510-c6d84d369be3" :step/math-academy-id 17171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060939 :tutorial/content "We will use mostly systems with the following variables:\n\n- $x,y,z,$ or\n\n- $x_1,x_2,x_3,$ or\n\n- $a,b,c.$\n\nThese sets of variables will always have a *natural* order:\n\n- alphabetical order in the case of $x,y,z$ or $a,b,c,$ and\n\n- numerical order in the case of $x_1, x_2, x_3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f6081e68-9014-5a0d-a6bf-084abe5fd28e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3147 :tutorial/title "The Order of the Variables in a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "17bd5ecf-ddf0-5755-a7bc-19c589337468" :step/math-academy-id 17172 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059578 :question/id #uuid "05942b5f-c165-59c6-8b67-24bc8dd40cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-4685"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7c8f4da-c782-51e0-ac51-87a89f31860d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409515}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the System of Equations Associated With an Augmented Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "9c9a13d7-cb68-5563-a0cd-305ad4cad7f7" :step/math-academy-id 17173 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059579 :question/id #uuid "161a4327-2356-5779-92a6-8495cfffefb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4686"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14d047c8-f032-5ff3-aced-65d40bfeeaad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338981}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a Linear System With Variables Other Than x, y, z"} :step/id #uuid "f6cdbf1b-5a64-5b3d-b66b-3ba956756491" :step/math-academy-id 17174}] :activity/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059882}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088695} :activity/id #uuid "388f5ace-d291-564e-ba4f-c8bd5e29bb8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050769 :topic/id #uuid "654e93d7-fd22-5247-81be-58bce3864517" :topic/math-academy-id 875} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087628 :question/id #uuid "11f5bedf-cfc8-5ace-b080-5ec30b406a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7436"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377900}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "86afef72-d424-5e00-9cca-2deb22614b51" :step/math-academy-id 27576 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087629 :question/id #uuid "29cfe1ff-ca64-51f9-8da1-5d0f798711f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7437"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049118}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377914}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "a81f2327-ee3f-53ff-8ff9-4e5727353be6" :step/math-academy-id 27577 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087802 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087630 :question/id #uuid "07678ad9-a16f-5fd2-8e39-85376aa9112e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7442"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377921}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"} :step/id #uuid "d0e480ce-44cf-566d-a958-fb2ee799eb46" :step/math-academy-id 27586 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088970 :tutorial/content "The standard parametric equations of a *vertical* parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nAs usual, the sign of $p$ determines whether the parabola is upward-opening or downward-opening.\n\n- When $p > 0,$ the parabola is upward-opening.\n\n![](images/39/3962fbc2a0fff44300677c89e867c57582473a8e56aedfe0fcf1d20fb9a322c9.png)\n\n- When $p < 0,$ the parabola is downward-opening.\n\n![](images/63/63acc881f875fbf96fb6ef8a8db9fb778669eabbc171e3800e521a48c130aa89.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0b858e3-ea45-5f93-903c-65d346ab9b0a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "283e44b0-129c-5ed3-8eda-f67b74ce0e0d" :step/math-academy-id 28491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088971 :tutorial/content "The parametric equations of a *horizontal* parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nThis time, the sign of $p$ determines whether the parabola is left-opening or right-opening.\n\n- When $p > 0,$ the parabola is right-opening.\n\n![](images/77/7758fc4d8eaec780a92c548393b48eb1457d6961fb33aa7e4e4e776e71a4fd7a.png)\n\n- When $p < 0,$ the parabola is left-opening.\n\n![](images/28/28f17924216890db2b0f9a6baf914502184ce21d7f5723a933c955e789b1bb23.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6480b59c-8f91-5c94-8eb9-0f1bfe519e81" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4625 :tutorial/title "The Parametric Equations of Horizontal Parabolas Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "3c903871-8fc1-5bba-b0a9-757980a0ad09" :step/math-academy-id 28492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088972 :tutorial/content "We've been told that the parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nBut how do we know that these parametric equations describe a parabola?\n\nFirst, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2pt & ⟹ &  & t & =\\frac{x}{2p} \\\\ y & =p{t}^{2} & ⟹ &  & {t}^{2} & =\\frac{y}{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first equation above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4{p}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the above into the second equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{y}{p},$ we can eliminate the parameter $t$ and rearrange to find the Cartesian equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{y}{p} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4{p}^{2}} & =\\frac{y}{p} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{y}{p}\\cdot 4{p}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4py\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the Cartesian equation of a vertical parabola, which confirms that the parametric equations describe a vertical parabola.\n\nIf we followed the same procedure for the parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0),$ we would obtain that the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 4px.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eeb3b2df-57fd-5962-bc60-e1e64961038e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4626 :tutorial/title "How Do We Know That the Parametric Equations Describe a Parabola?"} :step/id #uuid "f517edbc-d6fc-5d91-ab9f-1f40acb5734e" :step/math-academy-id 28495}] :activity/title "Parametric Equations of Parabolas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087940}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079398} :activity/id #uuid "640455b0-fd95-58ca-837b-957e478e8267" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050868 :topic/id #uuid "6cc04ddb-ad7b-51e8-8f9d-c71c72f8e9d4" :topic/math-academy-id 684} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079608 :tutorial/content "In general, a geometric series with common ratio $r$ is\n\n- convergent if $|r| <1,$ and\n\n- divergent if $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nFor example, consider the series \n\n$$\n4+2+1+\\dfrac 12+ \\cdots .\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $|r|<1$ and therefore the series converges. (This makes sense intuitively, because the magnitude of the terms keeps getting smaller.)\n\nOn the other hand, consider the series\n\n$$\n1-3+9-27+\\cdots .\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{(-3)}{1}=-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $|r|>1$ and therefore the series diverges. (This makes sense intuitively, because the magnitude of the terms keeps getting bigger.)\n\n**Note:** If $|r|=1,$ then the series diverges. For example, consider the series\n\n$$\n0.001+0.001+0.001+0.001+\\cdots .\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{0.001}{0.001}=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $|r|=1$ and therefore the series diverges. (This makes sense intuitively, because the terms are not approaching $0.$)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0187c3c-3b81-549c-8390-b4b5e692396b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "42573c7d-7d3c-51b7-8c10-a99b2209cdcd" :step/math-academy-id 2369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079399}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079399 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078491 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078345 :question/id #uuid "e623670b-6132-5b91-bd5e-4a52716f1d8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-689"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2ac075-4b9b-52d3-b053-84fe2d1b6545" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050807}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402723}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges"} :step/id #uuid "b5b04e34-fb3a-5f4c-864a-e3d0b081f3ad" :step/math-academy-id 2370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079400}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079400 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078492 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078347 :question/id #uuid "a8b61e74-1a6d-5efb-bda9-f91aca63d0d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-691"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348084}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index"} :step/id #uuid "d856b00a-c2fd-53a4-bf93-0f6cb8aa4e4e" :step/math-academy-id 2374 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079401}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078493 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078348 :question/id #uuid "454e2614-06fe-5fcd-b9cd-856e544f7d1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-693"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402716}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"} :step/id #uuid "51373666-f264-50dc-96ae-ac738e09974b" :step/math-academy-id 2378 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079406}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078497 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078343 :question/id #uuid "8ddc5edb-0181-50c7-9d1a-8ee206f1c08b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6773"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048553}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348085}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Range of Values of a Parameter for Which a Geometric Series Converges"} :step/id #uuid "0adff976-463c-54aa-86a5-005bccde425f" :step/math-academy-id 25027}] :activity/title "Convergence of Geometric Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078697}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055553} :activity/id #uuid "8a7c3e16-7b5a-517e-b02a-29c35f81f008" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056044 :tutorial/content "Given two sets $A$ and $B,$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we say that $A$ is a **subset** of $B,$ and we write \n\n$$\nA \\subseteq B.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ {\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{3}} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1,2,3\\}, \\qquad C=\\{1,2,{\\color{red}4}\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that *every* element of $A$ is also an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}}\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}}\\in B\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is a subset of $B,$ and we write $A \\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $A$ and $B$ as shown below. Notice that $A$ is contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/91/911dbbe9a638810f3a245fdcd004e10aee4dbd49fd8a99741c1bdd5c48cc2319.png)\n\nOn the other hand, not every element of $C$ is an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n1 \\in B, \\qquad 2\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{red}4}\\notin B\n$$\n\nSince $4 \\notin B,$ we have that $C$ is *not* a subset of $B,$ and we write $C \\not\\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $B$ and $C$ as follows. Notice that $C$ is *not* contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/45/454c28bacb8d5117bb11f76988b70344b4521d260adf2b690a11e7dfd2964385.png)\n\nAnother way of thinking about the statement $C \\not\\subseteq B$ is that $C$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $B.$ In this case, that element is $4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ef02f03-d232-5ab1-88fc-1f363535caa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8240 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a4c7c6c6-5b36-53d1-a4de-4c76a981a433" :step/math-academy-id 152757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056055 :tutorial/content "We note the following points regarding subsets:\n\n- Every set $A$ is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\nA\\subseteq A\n$$\n\nThis is because $A$ contains the same elements as $A,$ and therefore $A$ is a subset of $A.$\n\n- The empty set is a subset of *every* set. So, for all sets $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq A.\n$$\n\nTo understand this, remember that $\\emptyset \\not\\subseteq A$ means that $\\emptyset$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $A.$ However, this cannot be true since $\\emptyset$ contains no elements! Thus, we must have $\\emptyset\\subseteq A.$\n\nIt's worth pointing out that the empty set is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFinally, note that a set $A$ is a **proper subset** of a set $B$ if $A \\subseteq B$ and $A \\neq B.$ In this case, we can write \n\n$$\nA \\subset B.\n$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}, \\qquad C = \\{ 3,2,1 \\}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a proper subset of $B,$ but $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B{:}$\n\n- Since every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we have that $A\\subseteq B.$ Also, $A\\neq B.$ Therefore, $A$ is a *proper* subset of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nA\\subset B\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $C\\subseteq B$ because every element of $C$ is an element of $B.$ However, $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B$ because $C=B.$ Therefore, we write \n\n$$\nC \\not\\subset B.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1acb16ee-d59d-5050-9848-9cfdb6e2ee6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8316 :tutorial/title "Some Remarks"} :step/id #uuid "9cee0391-700f-550d-9b6e-139dae503e1e" :step/math-academy-id 152758 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054442 :question/id #uuid "70291b7a-ae46-5e8a-b24b-3ea343eb84f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395707}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"} :step/id #uuid "b812cc2f-347b-5ed3-979d-e5bff1beb134" :step/math-academy-id 152759 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056047 :tutorial/content "We can systematically list all the subsets of a given set using a tree diagram.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find all the subsets of the set $A=\\{ 0,1 \\}.$\n\nWe list every possible combination of elements within the set using a tree diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd51f5e02c9f280b8478e11497602dd7ae886b84d1d5994d8af73dff9719d20e.png)\n\nWe start with the empty set as the root $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}}.$ \n\nNext, we consider the element $0\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset.\n\n- If we don't include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we are left with the empty set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we get the set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}.$\n\nFinally, the list of all subsets is shown in the last column:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "daebe910-6059-595b-a45a-fc890a9b1b53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8244 :tutorial/title "Listing All the Subsets of a Set"} :step/id #uuid "547755c4-e17d-56bf-a674-91a455f0dbbf" :step/math-academy-id 152763 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054443 :question/id #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13196"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408073}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"} :step/id #uuid "2e361262-f159-54e6-a942-659d5017ac7b" :step/math-academy-id 152764 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056056 :tutorial/content "Recall that the natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, and real and complex numbers can be arranged in the following hierarchical diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66260e4c239137cd30453d9335256fd7d84e93a197612d670c19d97e417bd2f0.png)\n\nIn other words, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Z},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Q},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{R},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{C}.$ We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}\n$$\n\nSince none of these sets are equivalent, we can also write\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z} \\subset \\mathbb{Q} \\subset \\mathbb{R} \\subset \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nSome restricted or extended special sets are subsets of others. For example:\n\n- $\\mathbb N$ is the set of natural numbers, and $\\mathbb N_0$ is the set of natural numbers *and* zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbb N\\subset \\mathbb N_0.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z$ is the set of integers, $\\mathbb Z^+$ is the set of positive integers (equivalent to $\\mathbb N$), and $\\mathbb Z^+_0$ is the set of positive integers and zero (equivalent to $\\mathbb N_0$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^+\\subset  \\mathbb Z_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Q^+$ is the set of positive rational numbers, $\\mathbb R^+$ is the set of positive real numbers, and $\\mathbb R$ is the set of real numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Q^+\\subset  \\mathbb R^+ \\subset \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nHowever, note that, for example, $\\mathbb Q \\not\\subset  \\mathbb R^+,$ since $0\\in\\mathbb Q$ yet $0\\notin \\mathbb R^+.$\n\nFrom these basic inclusions, we can now deduce some subset relationships for other special numerical sets. For example, recall that [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] represents the set of all non-negative integers. \n\n* Obviously, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}[/math] since all the elements of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] are integers but there are some integers, say, [math]-1,[/math] that does not belong to [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]\n\n* On the other hand, [math]\\mathbb{N}[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] since all natural number lie in [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] but [math]0 \\notin \\mathbb{N}.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math]\n\nTherefore, we have [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]" :tutorial/id #uuid "c336106d-52a5-5d56-a98f-c64cd26eef4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8317 :tutorial/title "Subsets of Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5386697a-6e4f-5619-83d7-53f1064c31f2" :step/math-academy-id 152767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054444 :question/id #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13197"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399131}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "053e7255-8298-5305-9c62-d263e5b3b5ba" :step/math-academy-id 152768 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056073 :tutorial/content "The notation \n\n$$\nA \\supseteq B\n$$\n\nreads \"$A$ is a **superset** of $B$\", and is equivalent to $B \\subseteq A.$ This is similar to how we can write either $x \\leq y$ or $y \\geq x$. The two notations mean the same thing. \n\nSimilarly, $A \\supset B$ reads \"$A$ is a **proper superset** of $B$,\" and is equivalent to $B \\subset A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d87be2be-dac5-52e2-bcbd-7c0ad0d17032" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9065 :tutorial/title "Superset Notation"} :step/id #uuid "d545e0c5-338a-5d32-8901-ef36ab148f6e" :step/math-academy-id 152771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054445 :question/id #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13198"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351173}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"} :step/id #uuid "7b56c42d-9c31-515e-ae69-cd2b8675b68c" :step/math-academy-id 152772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056075 :tutorial/content "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n> *Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> S = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n> $$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nThe issue, unknown to mathematicians until later, is that the object [math]S[/math] is not a set. If it were, Russell's paradox would not occur since we should always be able to determine whether an object is a member of a set or not.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set. \n\n* If [math]S\\notin S,[/math] then by the definition of [math]S[/math] and applying De Morgan's law for conjunctions, we have\n\\begin{align*}\nS\\notin S \\Leftrightarrow \\lnot (S\\subseteq U\\land  S\\notin S)  \\equiv (S\\not\\subseteq U) \\lor (S\\in S).\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e2d16c6-3382-5ba2-8d4b-949482eb4f07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9388 :tutorial/title "Russell's Paradox: Why We Need a Universal Set"} :step/id #uuid "7280da1b-4410-550f-9a00-d46456526bfc" :step/math-academy-id 164820}] :activity/title "Subsets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054909}]}
